[DeadSpace1Patch]

; --- main-menu checkpoint changer --------------------------------------
; Press F9 in the main menu to open the tools overlay. TAB switches between
; the checkpoint browser and the randomizer setup page. The latter can edit
; the enemy seed and save safe presets for the next game launch; runtime hooks
; are deliberately never switched inside a loaded world. Use the arrow and
; page keys to select an authored checkpoint, then Enter/F8 to apply it.
; Close the browser with F9 before starting New Game
; to the next New Game in an unused save slot. This does not teleport a
; running game or modify an existing save. 120 is the Windows VK code for F9.
CheckpointChanger=1
CheckpointChangerHotkey=120

; --- deterministic campaign progression randomizer ----------------------
; Reads a seed manifest generated by trainer/generate_ds1_progression_manifest.py.
; This changes the Chapter 1 store weapon/ammo choices and all 16 schematic
; rewards at their native decode/init boundaries. Existing PlayerStore save
; entries are never rewritten. Starting-suit randomization is not enabled yet.
ProgressionRandomizer=1

; Randomizes the weapon a world pickup grants. Only three weapons
; exist as a world entity - Plasma Cutter, Line Gun, Force Gun - so
; this rotates those three; everything else is store-only.
; The chapter 1 maintenance bay is the one that matters.
FirstWeaponRandomizer=1

; CHEAT, for testing only. Makes EVERY pickup in the world grant a
; weapon, so a checkpoint start can be armed by walking over the first
; ammo box you see. Loading a checkpoint gives you nothing, and the
; debug menu cannot hand out weapons - the inventory field it would
; have to write was never identified.
; 0 = off, 1 = Plasma Cutter, 2 = Line Gun, 3 = Force Gun.
; Only those three exist as a world pickup at all; every other weapon
; is store-only and cannot be placed in the world.
; This also overwrites schematics and key items, so turn it back to 0
; before playing a run you want to keep.
CheatPickupWeapon=1

; Rotates the twelve upgrade node ids of every bench. The bench names
; its node types in plain text - damage, speed, capacity, width,
; reload, hp, air, energy, duration, range, charge - and carries an id
; beside each. Whether that id is the effect or just a key could not
; be settled by reading the executable, so the hook logs all twelve
; once before rotating: identical ids would mean there is nothing to
; rotate.
UpgradeBenchRandomizer=1

; Randomizes the weighted loot tables. ItemDropList is what decides
; what falls out of necromorphs and crates, and there is one table per
; combat area - roughly three hundred in the game, drawing on a pool of
; only sixteen distinct items. Each table holds up to sixteen entries
; of item plus relative weight (measured tables run 75/25 and
; 5/1/23/1).
; LootRandomizer rotates which item sits in which slot, so the common
; drop of a table becomes a different item.
; LootWeightRandomizer rotates the weights instead, which keeps the
; items but changes which of them is common and which is rare.
; Both can be on at once. A table always randomizes the same way, so
; reloading a checkpoint does not reroll the loot.
LootRandomizer=1
LootWeightRandomizer=1

; Draws each entry from the whole game's item set instead of shuffling
; the table's own items, so an area can drop something it never held.
; Counted offline: 608 tables use only eighteen item ids, and the twelve
; used in fourteen tables or more appear in every chapter, so those
; twelve form the pool. The six that appear once to five times are left
; out - handing one to a level that never streams it would produce a
; pickup the game cannot resolve.
; This overrides LootRandomizer for the items themselves;
; LootWeightRandomizer still applies on top.
LootGlobalPool=1

; How often a drop table yields nothing at all. Every table carries a
; weight for "nothing" beside its item weights, and the game rolls
; against the sum of both - the shipped tables always add up to a
; hundred, so that weight is a plain percentage.
; LootScarcity shifts it by whole percentage points across the whole
; game: +20 means twenty points more often empty-handed, -20 means
; twenty points less. 0 leaves the tables as authored. A table can
; never be pushed past 90 percent empty.
; LootScarcityRandom ignores that number and takes one value from the
; seed instead, so a run has a character: somewhere between fifteen
; points more generous and thirty-five points leaner, the same
; everywhere for that whole run.
LootScarcity=0
LootScarcityRandom=1

; Every necromorph carries ten per-limb values: how much damage that
; body part takes before it comes off. A stock Slasher authors
; 10 10 10 15 15 2 10 10 10 10 - one part gives way almost at once, and
; that is the weak spot every player learns.
; LimbWeakSpotRandomizer keeps the enemy's own ten numbers and only
; reorders them, so the weak spot moves to a different body part while
; the enemy stays exactly as tough overall.
; LimbProfilePool goes further and hands the enemy a profile measured
; from a different species, so a Slasher can come apart like something
; else entirely. It overrides the setting above. Start with the weak
; spot alone; turn this on when you want the bigger change.
; Neither touches bosses, story characters or props: only the twenty
; ordinary profiles measured across all archives are recognised, and
; anything else is left exactly as the developers authored it.
LimbWeakSpotRandomizer=1
LimbProfilePool=0

; The second per-limb list: how much overall health the necromorph
; loses when that part comes off. A Slasher authors 30 40 40 30 30, so
; taking the right limb costs it a lot more than the wrong one.
; Rotating these moves that payoff to different body parts, which
; changes where it is worth aiming without changing how hard anything
; is to cut off. Works alongside the two settings above and respects
; the length each enemy declares - a Slasher has ten sever values but
; only five costs.
LimbSeverCostRandomizer=1

; Isaac's air budget. The game authors sixty seconds once, in
; global_assets, which is why every vacuum section feels identical on a
; replay. With this on, the run draws one budget from the seed between
; twenty-five and a hundred and twenty seconds and keeps it for the
; whole game, so the air is something you learn rather than something
; you already know. Refills and air cannisters are untouched.
; This also covers the rooms the vacuum hook empties itself.
OxygenRandomizer=1

; What is inside the crates that hold a guaranteed item. Counted across
; all archives: 585 crates, 509 of which just roll a loot table, and 76
; with fixed contents - 37 of those hold a Power Node, which is to say
; the game's upgrade nodes have fixed hiding places.
; This trades those contents among the crates themselves, so the same
; items exist in the same numbers, only somewhere else. Nothing new is
; introduced, so no crate can hold something its level cannot load.
; Two crates are left alone: one holds an audio log and one holds an
; item that could not be identified.
; The log reports how many crates it recognised. If it says none were
; identified, the game does not keep entity ids in the object and the
; feature cannot work - tell me and nothing will have been changed.
CrateRandomizer=1

; Corpses that get up. A necromorph either climbs out of a vent or is
; already lying in the room waiting for you to come close - the game
; ships fourteen of the latter and they are all Zombies, so only Zombie
; spawners are touched here. Measured across all archives: 424 vent
; spawners, of which 140 can be addressed individually, and those are
; the candidates.
; PlayingDeadPercent is how many of them lie down instead, chosen from
; the seed and fixed for the run, so a body never turns back into a
; vent when you reload. Twenty is a good start: enough that you stop
; trusting corpses, rare enough that it keeps working.
; A handful of anchors cover several spawners at once, which reads as a
; room with several bodies in it rather than one.
PlayingDeadCorpses=1
PlayingDeadPercent=20

; Enhanced exploders. The exploder is the only creature the game never
; varies - all 59 of them share one tuning of five numbers - so this is
; the one variant that is genuinely ours rather than borrowed from
; somewhere else in the game.
; EnhancedExploderPercent decides how many of them get it, chosen from
; the seed and fixed for the run.
; EnhancedExploderStrength scales all five values at once (100 = stock,
; 150 = half again, capped at 300). All five are scaled together on
; purpose: which of them is blast radius and which is damage could not
; be settled by reading the executable, and a uniform scale cannot turn
; the creature into something incoherent.
; They do not look different yet - that needs a second character asset,
; which the game has for slasher, leaper, lurker and brute but not for
; the exploder.
EnhancedExploders=1
EnhancedExploderPercent=50
EnhancedExploderStrength=150
; Leave this off. The authored sup_expl donor already carries the
; stronger numbers and is swapped in per spawn. This older pass works
; per authored template, and one template covers every exploder in a
; level, so it turns all of them super at once.
EnhancedExploderRuntimeStats=0

; How much bigger an enhanced exploder is, in per cent (100 leaves the
; size alone, 60 to 140 allowed). This is the only visual tell available
; without a second character asset - the game ships one for slasher,
; leaper, lurker and brute, but not for the exploder.
; The transform is only touched when it still looks like a plain
; rotation, so nothing already scaled gets scaled twice. If the log says
; "not a plain rotation", the size is set somewhere else and this needs
; a different hook point.
; EnhancedExploderScale was removed: it never worked - the
; creature's transform is not where it was looked for - and the
; way it retried was unsafe, writing into objects that could
; already have been freed. That was the memory corruption behind
; the random crashes.

; EXPERIMENT, not a feature. An entity names its character asset in
; plain text and keeps a pointer to that text at +0x668. This points an
; enhanced exploder at a different creature that the run already
; streams, to find out whether the model can be swapped that way at all
; - the code that reads the pointer has not been found yet.
; 0 = off, 1 = leaper, 2 = lurker, 3 = twitcher, 4 = slasher,
; 5 = super_slasher.
; Expect one of three outcomes: nothing changes (the name is resolved
; earlier and this approach is dead), the creature looks wrong or
; T-posed (the skeleton does not match, but the lever works), or it
; renders as that creature (the lever works and a character asset of
; our own becomes possible). Any of the three is an answer.
; Only the enhanced half is affected, so you can compare side by side.
ExploderModelProbe=0
ProgressionRandomizerDiagnostics=1
ProgressionManifest=DeadSpace1Progression.tsv

; Randomizes only the two weapons unlocked by the Chapter 1 starting store.
; The Plasma Cutter is always retained and ammunition follows the selected
; weapons. The exact StartNewGame_Menu path arms one bounded memory patch;
; loading an existing save never opens that gate.
StartingStoreRandomizer=1

; Explicit rescue mode for a save that reached the first shop before the
; starting-store randomizer was installed. It only accepts the exact vanilla
; Chapter 1 store list. Enable for one load/save cycle, then disable again.
ExistingSaveStoreMigration=0

; Reports every single item the game puts into the PlayerStore, with
; the address of whoever asked. Pure observation, changes nothing.
; Open a shop once with this on and the log says who fills it.
StoreDiagnostics=1

; Diagnostic only: records every native UniformManager apply call, including
; requested/current table index, uniform GUID, inventory slots and damage
; factor. It never selects a suit or writes save data. Enable for the short
; New Game / save-load trace used to build the starting-suit randomizer.
StartingSuitDiagnostics=1

; The native StartNewGame_Menu path arms exactly one level-1 uniform request,
; which is redirected deterministically using EnemyRandomizerSeed.
; Levels 2-6 are used so every test produces a visible change. The next test
; establishes whether the selected index is restored natively after a full
; process restart; do not use this on an important existing save yet.
StartingSuitRandomizer=1
StartingSuitIncludeDefault=0

; Default for any enemy class without a line of its own below.
; 1 is the game as shipped, 3 means a lone slasher arrives as three.
SpawnsPerTrigger=1

; --- experimental, safe-template enemy randomizer ------------------------
; OFF by default.  The game still performs every spawn itself.  Once an
; authored FuncSpawn target has produced a recognised enemy, the patch may
; temporarily exchange only its target GUID with another *currently loaded*
; target from the same proven actor family, then restores it before the call
; returns.  That preserves the level's trigger, position and vent/script
; behaviour.  It never free-spawns actors. Without the global options it uses
; only targets currently owned by the loaded level.
;
; Reviewed runtime candidates: Zombie (Slasher/Twitcher/Super Slasher),
; Lurker, Leaper and Exploder. Bosses, Guardians, bodies, tentacles, Hunter
; and story NPCs are excluded by design. With ContextV1 + both global options,
; 227 known FuncSpawns can randomize on their first firing; unknown sources
; remain authored instead of waiting for runtime learning.
EnemyRandomizer=0
EnemyRandomizerChancePercent=100
; Stable 32-bit seed. Decimal and 0x-prefixed hexadecimal values are accepted.
; The decision is derived only from this seed and the authored source/target
; GUIDs, so checkpoint reloads reproduce the same enemy for the same spawner.
EnemyRandomizerSeed=0x00D5A11
; Use the generated FuncSpawn/context table. Vent ceiling, side-wall, floor
; and angled entries are separate contracts. Unknown, scripted, zero-G,
; corpse and unreviewed spawn styles fail closed and keep their authored enemy.
EnemyRandomizerContextV1=1
EnemyRandomizerForcedVariant=
; OFF by default. When enabled, permits the reviewed cross-class pairs that
; share an authored spawn context. With 0, V1 uses only the 35 same-class
; rules; this is the recommended first-test setting.
EnemyRandomizerCrossFamilyContexts=0
; A failed replacement may already have fired script/resolver side effects.
; Replaying an alternate and then the authored event made one trigger execute
; up to three times and is unsafe across checkpoints. Keep this 0 for normal
; play; 1 exists only for controlled diagnostics.
EnemyRandomizerRetryFailedSpawns=0
; Logs only first learned templates and replacement decisions.  Keep this 0
; for normal play: it adds no per-frame logging.
EnemyRandomizerDiagnostics=0
; EXPERIMENTAL GLOBAL STAGE: allow the collision-free ordinary-enemy sidecar
; targets after every one has registered in SimManager. Candidate class is
; checked again by the game's real factory; any mismatch retries the untouched
; authored target. Supported randomization remains limited to the compatible
; families listed above while the spawn-style table is being completed.
EnemyRandomizerGlobalTemplates=1

; Load and retain only the character/resource archives and retail audio banks
; required by the currently enabled candidate policy, plus the sanitised
; target sidecar, through DS1's native StreamManager. Character BNKs do not
; contain all streamed voices/attacks, hence the separate audio stage.
; Both options must be 1. Any resource, target or class failure leaves the
; original enemy untouched. This is memory-heavy and remains OFF by default.
EnemyRandomizerGlobalStreaming=1

; EXPERIMENTAL CROSS-FAMILY STAGE: replaces a learned Pregnant target only
; with a currently loaded, verified Zombie target. The original Pregnant
; FuncSpawn still controls the authored location, trigger and vent. It does
; not load external models; a mismatch is rejected and retried as Pregnant.
; This is the safe foundation for Pregnant -> Super Slasher once the archive
; scan identifies which Zombie template owns the Super Slasher package.
EnemyRandomizerCrossFamilyGround=0

; Passive research recorder.  It has no effect on spawning or randomization:
; while you play normally, it writes one deduplicated source UID / authored
; target UID / resulting actor-class row to DeadSpace1Patch_SpawnCatalog.tsv.
; This is intentionally separate from diagnostics, so it is safe to leave on
; for an entire playthrough without combat-log spam or a per-frame scan.
SpawnCatalog=1

; Deeper, still deduplicated research only. It records the actual runtime
; resolver object for each unique source/target pair; keep it 0 unless we are
; investigating global templates.
SpawnResolverDiagnostics=1

; How many of each kind arrives on a trigger. Not a percentage: the number is
; the total, so 4 means four of them where the level placed one. 1 leaves that
; kind exactly as the game shipped it, and 0 does the same.
;
; Only spawns that build an actual enemy are touched at all. The patch watches
; which class the game constructs, so corpses, props, weapons and scenery are
; never multiplied. Names come from the game's own class table.

; --- the ordinary necromorphs -------------------------------------------
EnemyType_Zombie            =1   ; Slasher, and also Twitcher and Wheezer:
                                 ; those are variants of the same class
EnemyType_Lurker            =1
EnemyType_Leaper            =1
EnemyType_Exploder          =1   ; two is already a lot of explosions
EnemyType_Infector          =1   ; each one turns corpses into more slashers
EnemyType_SwarmEnemy        =1

; --- the heavy ones ------------------------------------------------------
EnemyType_Brute             =1
EnemyType_Pregnant          =1   ; bursts into swarmers when cut open
EnemyType_DividerCore       =1

; --- set pieces and parts, better left alone -----------------------------
EnemyType_Hunter            =1   ; the regenerator; cannot be killed, only
                                 ; slowed. More than one is a different game.
EnemyType_Guardian          =1   ; fixed to a wall, more of them only annoys
EnemyType_GuardianPod       =1
EnemyType_GuardianTentacle  =1
EnemyType_DividerPart       =1   ; the pieces a divider breaks into
EnemyType_StaticEnemy       =1   ; bodies that wake up when you walk past
EnemyType_GenericNPC        =1   ; crew, not necromorphs
EnemyType_NeutralNPC        =1
EnemyType_Slug              =1   ; the asteroid gun sequence
EnemyType_SlugTentacle      =1
EnemyType_HiveMind          =1   ; final boss
EnemyType_HiveMindEye       =1
EnemyType_Leviathan         =1   ; chapter 9 boss
EnemyType_LeviathanTentacle =1
EnemyType_DragTentacle      =1   ; the one that drags you down a corridor
EnemyType_SlamDragTentacle  =1

; =========================================================================
; Placement readout
; =========================================================================
; Press F10 in a loaded level. The log gets your own position and viewing
; angle plus every authored spawner nearby with its distance, its name, what
; it points at (Wand/Decke/Boden) and how many enemies it is allowed to make.
; That is where the names and the coordinates below come from.
; 121 is the Windows VK code for F10; set 0 to switch the readout off.
PlacementHotkey=121

; =========================================================================
; Vacuum zones
; =========================================================================
; Adds the native vacuum scene flag to selected ZoneRender volumes. The game
; keeps control of oxygen, audio and post-processing. Press F10 with
; diagnostics enabled to log the current ZoneRender GUID.
VacuumZones=1
VacuumZoneDiagnostics=1

; =========================================================================
; Engine noclip
; =========================================================================
; F7 toggles noclip in a loaded level. The arrow keys move Isaac relative to
; his facing direction. Page Up and Page Down move vertically, and Shift
; triples the speed. Movement goes through CharacterMovementComponent so the
; Havok controller and the visible player transform stay together.
NoClip=1
NoClipHotkey=118
NoClipSpeedCm=50

; =========================================================================
; [ExtraSpawns] - enemies the level never had
; =========================================================================
; One line per placement, in the [ExtraSpawns] section at the end of this
; file:
;
;   <spawner name> = <variant>, <count>, <percent> [, <x>, <y>, <z> [, <yaw> [, <mode>]]]
;
; The spawner name is an authored trigger; F10 lists the ones around you.
; "any" instead of a name applies the rule to every spawner that fires.
; <percent> is rolled from the randomizer seed, so a checkpoint reload
; reproduces the same result.
;
; The three optional numbers are a world position of your own. Without them
; the enemy appears where the spawner is - right for a shaft if the enemy can
; climb out of one, wrong for a Guardian, who would sit inside the vent.
; Stand where you want it, press F10, copy your player position from the log.
;
; <yaw> is the facing in degrees around the vertical axis. Without it the
; enemy looks wherever the borrowed spawner points, which is into the vent.
; F10 prints your own facing in exactly this notation.
;
; <mode> asks for a particular kind of donor:
;   dead    the enemy is already lying in the room as a corpse and gets up
;           when you come close. Nine such enemies exist in the whole game
;           and this borrows them; there is no way to make an ordinary one
;           behave that way, because it is a property of the target.
;           Available for: slasher, twitcher, super_slasher, exploder,
;           leaper. Asking for anything else finds no donor and the log
;           says so instead of quietly spawning a normal enemy.
;   vent    force a shaft donor
;   direct  force a placed donor
; Leave it empty and the place decides.
;
; Whether the enemy fits the place is checked: a shaft trigger looks for a
; shaft donor first. If the variant has none, the spawn still happens and the
; log says "mismatched donor" - that is when the rule needs its own position.
;
; Variants, exactly as the pool names them: zombie and zombievar1 (both
; ordinary slashers), zombiewoman, twitcher, super_slasher, exploder,
; leaper, super_leaper, lurker, super_lurker, pregnant, hunter, guardian,
; infector, super_brute.
; Of those, these have a playing-dead donor and can take mode 'dead':
; zombie, zombievar1, twitcher, super_slasher, exploder, leaper.

; --- the old single-placement switches, still read -----------------------
; Superseded by [ExtraSpawns] but kept working. ExtraSpawnSource takes one
; spawner name or GUID, or "any". Leave ExtraSpawnVariant empty to disable.
ExtraSpawnSource=
ExtraSpawnVariant=
ExtraSpawnCount=1
ExtraSpawnChancePercent=100

; --- Zone probe ----------------------------------------------------------
; F12 reports which ZoneRender zone you are standing in and logs its
; property block. Read-only while ZoneProbeOffset is 0.
; To experiment, set ZoneProbeOffset to an offset from the log (decimal,
; 256 to 540) and ZoneProbeValue to the raw value; the next F12 writes it
; into the zone you are in. Note that all 390 zones in the game are
; authored identically, so there is little here to change.
; F11 lists the world objects around you, F12 the zone you stand in.
; Both need this switch; without it the two keys do nothing.
WorldEditor=1
WorldEditorHotkey=122
ZoneProbeHotkey=123
ZoneProbeOffset=494
ZoneProbeValue=0

; --- Level order ---------------------------------------------------------
; Every chapter transition goes through one function, 0x00401EC0, which
; copies the folder and archive name into the pending load. Measured:
; a real transition comes from LevelChange (0x005B67B3), while starting a
; game or restoring a save comes from the generic caller (0x005F76B8).
; Diagnostics=1 only logs which caller asked for what.
; Randomizer=1 redirects real transitions only.
LevelOrderDiagnostics=1
LevelOrderRandomizer=0

